WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.528
A hook is your own code that runs at a set point in the agent's lifecycle, every time.

00:00:05.878 --> 00:00:07.883
No model decides whether it runs.

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:10.678
That makes hooks the only layer that enforces.

00:00:11.028 --> 00:00:14.697
Use them for what must always happen: blocking, formatting, logging.

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:19.503
The lifecycle has points like session start, prompt submitted,

00:00:19.503 --> 00:00:21.921
before a tool runs, after it runs, and stop.

00:00:22.271 --> 00:00:24.319
Claude Code has thirty-three of them.

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:30.323
A hook can be a shell command, an HTTP call, an MCP tool, a prompt, or another agent.

00:00:30.673 --> 00:00:34.214
Before a tool runs, a command that exits with code two blocks it.

00:00:34.564 --> 00:00:39.108
Each hook receives the event as JSON, like the exact command about to run.

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:43.149
And hooks from every scope all fire. They add up; they don't override.

00:00:43.499 --> 00:00:46.272
Run /hooks to see every hook that's configured.

00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:49.997
This one runs before every shell command.

00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:52.061
If the command is a force push,

00:00:52.061 --> 00:00:54.550
it prints the reason and exits with code two.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.197
The push never happens,

00:00:56.197 --> 00:00:59.017
and the agent reads the reason and changes course.

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.981
The same pattern can block edits to secrets,

00:01:01.981 --> 00:01:04.061
or format a file after every write.

00:01:05.461 --> 00:01:08.228
A rule that says never edit .env is a request.

00:01:08.228 --> 00:01:10.453
A hook that blocks it is a guarantee.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.751
But prove it fires. In the setup behind this series,

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:16.755
the guard hooks sat in an example file and never ran.

00:01:17.105 --> 00:01:19.857
Hooks cost no context unless they print something.

00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:24.367
Codex has hooks too, in hooks.json, with a smaller set of events.
